{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'func Test.*(Delivery.*Carr|Carried|Engine)' pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_validation_run_test.go pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_engine_identity_test.go; sed -n '75,114p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/spec_trailers.go; rg -n 'validate --carry|selfContained' POSE.md | cut -c 1-1000\"","aggregated_output":"pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_engine_identity_test.go:12:func TestEngineIdentityRefusesModifiedBuilds(t *testing.T) {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_validation_run_test.go:7:func TestDeliveryValidationRunReadsTheCarriedHead(t *testing.T) {\nfunc RawHistoryGit(root string, args ...string) *exec.Cmd {\n\treturn gitCommand(append([]string{\"--no-replace-objects\", \"-C\", root}, args...)...)\n}\n\n// RawHistoryGitContext is RawHistoryGit under ctx.\nfunc RawHistoryGitContext(ctx context.Context, root string, args ...string) *exec.Cmd {\n\treturn gitCommandContext(ctx, append([]string{\"--no-replace-objects\", \"-C\", root}, args...)...)\n}\n\n// GitHooksDir is the directory git runs the hooks of root from: a linked worktree shares the\n// one of its common directory, and core.hooksPath moves it. Where git cannot answer it is\n// `.git/hooks`, which is where a repository would keep them.\nfunc GitHooksDir(root string) string {\n\tout, err := RawHistoryGit(root, \"rev-parse\", \"--git-path\", \"hooks\").Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn filepath.Join(root, \".git\", \"hooks\")\n\t}\n\tdir := strings.TrimSuffix(string(out), \"\\n\")\n\tif filepath.IsAbs(dir) {\n\t\treturn dir\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(root, dir)\n}\n\n// RawGit is RawHistoryGit for a caller whose arguments already say where to read.\nfunc RawGit(args ...string) *exec.Cmd {\n\treturn gitCommand(append([]string{\"--no-replace-objects\"}, args...)...)\n}\n\n// SpecTrailerCommits reads the history of root and returns the commits that carry the trailer of\n// slug, the way the attribution of change sets does. An error is a history that could not be\n// read, which is not a history without commits.\nfunc SpecTrailerCommits(root, slug string) ([]string, error) {\n\tout, err := RawHistoryGit(root, SpecTrailerLogArgs...).Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\treturn ParseSpecTrailerLog(out)[slug], nil\n}\n353:  **Changed scope:** `--changed-from <rev> [--changed-to <rev>]` selects the minimum safe module set deterministically — modules containing changed files (tracked and untracked), transitive dependents via `dependsOn` edges in [`module-metadata.json`](.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json), and policy widening (criticality `high` always runs). Changed names are read NUL-delimited and a rename names both its source and its destination. Each changed file selects its most specific owning module, every module enclosing that owner (the root module encloses them all), every module beneath it (a gitlink or replaced directory changes what lies under it), and every module whose declared `dependsOn` contains it, lies beneath it or is the root `.`, even after that dependency is deleted. A file no module owns selects the root module. Module metadata that does not decode stops the selection (a full run under `--carry`, an error under `--changed-from`). A change outside every module runs every mod\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
